Here is a simple C++ program that finds the nth Fibonacci number using recursion:

```c++
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main()
{
    int n, x;
    cout << "Enter the number of Fibonacci numbers you want to calculate: ";
    cin >> n;
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++)
    {
        x =  fibonacci(i);
        cout << "The n-th Fibonacci number is: " << x << endl;
    }
    return 0;
}

int fibonacci(int n)
{
    if (n == 0 || n == 1)
    {
        return 1;
    }
    else if (n <= 2)
    {
        return fibonacci(n - 1) + fibonacci(n - 2);
    }
    else
    {
        return fibonacci(n - 1);
    }
}
```
This program takes `n` as the number of Fibonacci numbers that we want to calculate. It then iterates through the loop, calling the `fibonacci()` function for each Fibonacci number. The `fibonacci()` function calculates the n-th Fibonacci number using recursion.

Inside the `fibonacci()` function, we check if the current element is zero or one. If it is, we return 1, which is the first Fibonacci number. If the element is greater than or equal to 2, we call `fibonacci()` with the previous two values and return the sum of the previous two values. This continues until we reach the last element, at which point, we return the last value.</s>

