Here is a Python function to find the highest common ancestor (HCA) of two nodes in a binary tree:

```python
def hca(root, p, q):
  if p == root or q == root:
    # These nodes are HCA
    return root
  else:
    lca = hca(root.left, p, q)
    uca = hca(root.right, p, q)
    if lca == root or uca == root:
      # This node is the HCA
      return root
    else:
      # These nodes have different parents
      return min(lca, uca)
```

This function takes the root of the binary tree and the two nodes that you want to find the HCA for as arguments. It will then recursively call the function to find the HCA of the node's left and right subtrees, and returns the minimum of these two values. If either of the nodes is the root of the tree, it means that they are the HCA and the function returns the root. If the two nodes have different parents, the function returns the minimum of the two values that it found in the subtrees. This ensures that the HCA of any node is always unique.</s>

